.TH KAGI-CONFIG 1 "September 2026" "Kagi 0.38.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kagi-config \- model Kagi failure domains, optimize chunk geometry, and emit a 60-bit placement plan
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kagi-config
[\fB--config\fR FILE | \fB--wizard\fR] [OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs Monte Carlo and deterministic analysis of concurrent site, rack, host, network and disk failures. It compares Reed-Solomon, LRC, MSR and CLAY chunk geometries and emits a placement salt, equal host ranges and validated deployment policies.
.PP
The minimum supported topology is six servers across at least two racks, with at least six disks per server. A policy must tolerate concurrent disk, host and rack failures. Site and network tolerance apply where those domains have multiple members. With exactly two sites, a policy may explicitly allow zero site failures after the operator types I ACCEPT THAT LOSING A SITE CAN MAKE OBJECTS UNAVAILABLE.
.SH WIZARD
.B --wizard
starts by asking whether to use quick defaults.
.PP
Quick mode asks for number of sites, racks per site, hosts per rack, and NVMe disks per host. It uses 8 TB NVMe disks, default failure and repair rates, and automatic EC geometry selection. Every host has two modeled network paths on distinct sequential /24 subnets from 10.0.0.0/8.
.PP
Answer no to receive the full questionnaire for disk classes, AFRs, capacities, site/rack CIDRs, multi-homing, host names, device identities, failure rates, repair behavior, and policy constraints.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -c ", " --config " FILE"
Read YAML topology/model configuration.
.TP
.B --wizard
Run the interactive quick/full topology wizard.
.TP
.B --wizard-config-output FILE
Save wizard input as reusable YAML. Default: kagi.wizard.yaml.
.TP
.B --mount-root DIR
Override the generated disk mount root.
.TP
.BR -o ", " --output " FILE"
Write generated plan YAML.
.TP
.B --trials N
Ordinary Monte Carlo trials per placement candidate.
.TP
.B --candidates N
Placement salts evaluated.
.TP
.B --seed N
Deterministic simulation seed.
.TP
.B --rare-trials N
Importance-sampling trials per finalist.
.TP
.B --finalists N
Ordinary-MC finalists evaluated with rare-event simulation.
.TP
.B --validation-keys N
Accepted for compatibility. Validation covers every distinct host-range layout.
.TP
.B --time-trials N
Continuous-time trajectories.
.TP
.B --horizon-years YEARS
Years per continuous-time trajectory.
.TP
.B --max-storage-overhead RATIO
Maximum stored fragment payload relative to object size for a candidate geometry.
.TP
.B --example-object-size-bytes BYTES
Object size used for storage overhead comparisons. Repeat to compare several sizes.
.TP
.B --chunk-replicas N
Store N physical copies of each encoded chunk.
.TP
.B --object-replicas N
Force N full-object copies instead of erasure coding.
.SH EC GEOMETRY OPTIMIZATION
For every policy, the output reports the lowest-loss feasible tested geometry for Reed-Solomon, LRC, MSR, and CLAY. If optimize.auto_geometry is true, the policy's selected protection is changed to the feasible family/geometry with the lowest simulated unreadable probability, with the configured objective used as the secondary criterion.
.PP
Automatic search is bounded by topology placement limits and defaults to no more than 32 total chunks. Set optimize.max_total_fragments to change this ceiling.
.SH OUTPUT
The YAML output contains infrastructure, optimizer results, per-schema geometry reports, keyspace.host_ranges, and policies.<name>.deployment. Reference the output and policy in the node YAML's planner_output setting. Disk mount names are generated as host-diskclass-ordinal under the configured mount root.
.SH FILES
.I kagi.join.key
contains a newly generated cluster admission secret unless overridden.
.SH SEE ALSO
kagi-cluster-host(1), kagi-host(1), kagi-install(1)
.SH LIVE PROGRESS
When standard error is an interactive terminal, the optimizer displays a continuously refreshed status panel. The panel shows overall phase progress, current-operation progress, cumulative simulation count and rate, elapsed time, the lowest-failure placement/keyspace candidate observed so far, and the lowest-failure erasure-code geometry observed so far.
.PP
Progress output is written to standard error. Generated YAML remains on standard output and in the file selected by
.BR --output .
.TP
.B --no-progress
Disable the interactive progress display.
.TP
.B --progress-interval-ms N
Set the progress refresh interval in milliseconds. Default: 150. Values are internally clamped to 50 through 2000 milliseconds.
.PP
Progress is automatically disabled when standard error is not a terminal.

.SH CUDA AND RUNTIME MODES
Build with the cuda, hip, or opencl feature and use --mc-backend auto, cpu, cuda, hip, or opencl. GPU execution accelerates batched EC readability scoring while topology/failure generation and deterministic validation remain CPU-side.
.PP
--run-mode quick30 and quick60 select reduced-confidence simulation workloads intended to complete substantially sooner than full mode. The names are approximate runtime classes, not completion-time guarantees.
.SH CHECKPOINTS
Use --checkpoint DIR to save normalized inputs, run settings, admission key and completed trial batches. --checkpoint-seconds defaults to 60 and --checkpoint-batch-trials defaults to 1024. Saves occur at completed batch boundaries and phase boundaries. Use --resume DIR to reuse saved settings and committed batches; conflicting simulation options and incompatible versions are rejected.
.SH SCHEDULING
Use kagi-run to select scheduling policy and CPU affinity before the planner starts its workers.
